    "q-138855": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138855",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x.$",
