    "q-341727": {
      "dom_id": "step-q341727",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $y=(2x+3)^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(2x+3)^{x-1}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(2x+3)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2x+3)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x+3}\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(2x+3)^{x}:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+3)^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}) \\\\  & =(2x+3)^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)}{2x+3}+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}) \\\\  & =(2x+3)^{x-1}\\cdot ((2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
