      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+1)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2x+1}}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y\\,(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=0} & ={(\\sqrt{2(0)+1})}^{(0)^{2}+1}(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)+1)+\\frac{(0)^{2}+1}{2(0)+1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
